 ],
 "literal": "Like the growing crescent moon, step by step increasing, / with the distressing pain that made the wrist-bangles slip, / becoming like a crushed soft leaf-shoot and wasting away, / not only suffering because those who are near are absent, / the rain also, O friend, has darkened and set in! / Even when the poured rain does not fall, / more than us who grieve on his account, / on our account grieves this bustling town!",
 "translation": "O friend, like the growing crescent moon, my distressing pain increases step by step, making my wrist-bangles slip. Wasted away, I have become like a crushed soft leaf-shoot. Not only do I suffer because he is not near, but the rain clouds have also darkened and set in! Even when the poured rain does not fall, this bustling town grieves for us, even more than we grieve for him!",
 "modern": "தோழியே! வளரும் பிறை நிலவைப்போல நாளுக்கு நாள் வளர்ந்து, என் மணிக்கட்டு வளையல்களைக் கழலச் செய்த துன்பம் தரும் நோயோடு, கசக்கிய தளிர் போலாகி நான் மெலிந்து கிடக்கிறேன். என் அருகில் அவர் இல்லாததால் நான் படும் துன்பம் ஒருபுறமிருக்க, மேகங்களும் இருண்டு மழையைப் பொழியத் தொடங்கிவிட்டன. பெய்ய வேண்டிய மழை பெய்யாத காலத்திலும், நாம் அவருக்காக வருந்துவதை விட, இந்த நடமாட்டம் மிகுந்த ஊர் நமக்காக அதிகமாக வருந்துகிறது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her friend during the rainy season, which is the designated time for the hero's return. She laments that her physical distress has grown steadily like a waxing moon, causing her to lose weight so that her bangles slip off, and making her look like a crushed leaf-shoot. To make matters worse, the dark rain clouds have set in, intensifying her longing. However, she notes a surprising change in her surroundings: the bustling townspeople, who are usually prone to gossip, are now showing deep sympathy for her state. They seem to grieve for her suffering even more than she herself grieves for the absent hero.",
